<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hunminjeongeum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1446">1446</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hunminjeongeum ('Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People') is the document by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a> of Joseon promulgated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Korean alphabet</a>, now called Hangul, in 1446. The alphabet of 28 letters, created in 1443, was designed so that ordinary people could write their own language. The original explanatory edition, the Hunminjeongeum Haerye, rediscovered in 1940, is inscribed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World register.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum.jpg" alt="Hunminjeongeum" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sejong the Great of Joseon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hunminjeongum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
